{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And these are the names of the children of Israel who came to Egypt.</b> He added a vav in the word 'וְאֵלֶּה,' and he said first 'הַבָּאִים' in the present tense, and afterward said 'אִישׁ וּבֵיתוֹ בָּאוּ' in the past tense — because it was stated above (Genesis 50:26), 'וַיִּישֶׂם בָּאָרוֹן בְּמִצְרָיִם,' and after the death of Yosef the faces of the Egyptians were no longer toward Israel as the day before, and they felt then the coming to Egypt as though they came at that moment to Egypt. Therefore it says 'וְאֵלֶּה,' adding to the earlier matter, for from the standpoint of Yosef's death it is as though they are now coming. But in any event, even though Yosef died, behold Yaakov did not die; therefore it says 'אֵת יַעֲקֹב' — for they were still with Yaakov, and the merit of Yaakov stood for them, and the merit of the fact that they were fenced off from forbidden relations and did not wish to marry the Egyptian women who were steeped in lust, and all of them married wives before their coming to Egypt — this is what it says: 'אִישׁ וּבֵיתוֹ בָּאוּ.'" "And the commentators said (Rabbenu Bachya on [Exodus 1]) 'אִישׁ וּבֵיתוֹ בָּאוּ' — 'אִישׁ,' this is the Holy One, blessed be He: 'אִישׁ מִלְחָמָה' (Exodus 15); 'וּבֵיתוֹ' — and His court, as it is written (Exodus 13:21), 'וַה׳ הוֹלֵךְ לִפְנֵיהֶם' — He and His court (Zohar fol. 46). And what is the relevance of His court here? And it appears that He came to assure them that would be fulfilled in them: 'וְגַם אֶת הַגּוֹי אֲשֶׁר יַעֲבֹדוּ דָּן אָנֹכִי' (Genesis 15:14). Therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, and His court came with them to assure them that HaShem would do battle for them in Egypt." "<b>And these are the names of the children of Israel.</b> Rashi explained: to make known their belovedness, for they are likened to the stars, etc. Some say that the star, even though it is not seen by day, is nevertheless in existence also by day; so too the righteous man in the World to Come — which is likened to the day — has existence and is not seen and is not lost except to his generation. And some say the opposite: the star is not seen except at night; so too the light of the righteous man shines after his sun has set, as it is stated (Ecclesiastes 12:2), 'עַד אֲשֶׁר לֹא תֶחְשַׁךְ הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ' etc. And to me it appears, since the essence of a person's good name is recognized only after his death — for in his lifetime it is not a known thing whether he will remain in his righteousness, as it is written (Ecclesiastes 4:2), 'וְשַׁבֵּחַ אֲנִי אֶת הַמֵּתִים' — for in his lifetime there is no place to recount his praise, for who knows whether he will remain in his righteousness. And this is what is said: 'וְיוֹסֵף הָיָה בְמִצְרָיִם' — Rashi explained that this comes to make known his righteousness, that he stood in his righteousness from beginning to end, for the end of every matter is what is heard; therefore his praise was recounted after 'וַיִּישֶׂם בָּאָרוֹן בְּמִצְרָיִם' (Genesis 50:26). And in this respect they were likened to stars, which are seen after the setting of the sun; so too after a person's sun has set, his righteousness is seen if he remained in it until his end. And this is what is written (Daniel 12:3): 'וּמַצְדִּיקֵי הָרַבִּים כַּכּוֹכָבִים לְעוֹלָם וָעֶד' — for one who makes the many righteous: no sin comes through him, so that he would not be in Gehennom while his disciple is in the Garden of Eden (Yoma 87a), and in him it is a definitive matter — his light shines like the stars forever and ever even after his death. But one who does not make the many righteous — it is not a definitive matter that he will remain in his righteousness forever." "And what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (see Exodus Rabbah 1:3) — that they were likened to stars, which He brings in and brings out by number — there is some hint to this from the fact that it says 'הַבָּאִים' in the present tense, and it is written 'אִישׁ וּבֵיתוֹ בָּאוּ' in the past tense — for it speaks of two comings: one, when they came to Egypt, 'אֵת יַעֲקֹב אִישׁ וּבֵיתוֹ' — they had already come; that first coming was with Yaakov, and every man came with his household, his wife. But now, when He wished to speak of their deaths — for this too is called a coming, and it is as though they are now coming, since they died there and remained there — this is a second coming, in the present tense. But not with Yaakov, for they died and Yaakov did not die. Also this coming is not 'אִישׁ וּבֵיתוֹ' together, for his wife and sons do not accompany him except to the grave, as is stated in a parable in Pirkei de-Rabbi Eliezer (chapter 34). Therefore it is not 'אִישׁ וּבֵיתוֹ.' And from this there is a proof that it speaks of entry and exit, for both are called a coming." "And the matter of the counting is to make known their greatness, for every important thing has a number, as will be explained with the help of HaShem, blessed be He, in the portion of Ki Tissa (Exodus 30:12). And concerning the greatness of the righteous man who has died, it is written (Psalms 56:9), 'נֹדִי סָפַרְתָּ אָתָּה, שִׂימָה דִמְעָתִי בְנֹאדֶךָ, הֲלֹא בְּסִפְרָתֶךָ,' and this verse our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Shabbat 105a) concerning one who brings tears upon a person for naught. But in any event, what it says — 'הֲלֹא בְּסִפְרָתֶךָ' — requires interpretation. And it appears that he wished to bring a wonder as proof of the greatness of the righteous man who has died — that he is not a lost thing but has substance — and he said: Are they not in Your count, for You bring them in by number? Therefore I ask that You also count my wanderings and place my tear in Your flask for safekeeping."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {3 "The citation 'יומא פז.' refers to Yoma 87a (the abbreviated page number following standard folio format)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:18:02.453279528Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}